  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Weather radar is depicting returns over\nportions of north-central and central Nevada this afternoon. This\nis associated with an upper disturbance/1.5PV anomaly that is \nmoving slowly overhead. The feature will continue on its eastward \njig through the late afternoon and evening hours with partial \nclearing expected from west to east. The near surface is moist and\nlocalized patchy freezing fog is possible in the valleys in the \nnorth, but current thinking is that enough cloud cover will remain\nand winds will not be light enough for freezing fog formation. \nOvernight lows will be in the teens and 20s.\n\nFor Wednesday, short wave ridging will be racing across the\nforecast area. There will be a brief lull in the active weather\nbut expect the shower activity to move into Humboldt county during\nthe afternoon hours. Out ahead of the system, breezy to locally\nwindy conditions are expected to develop in central Nevada with\nsoutherly winds 15 to 25 mph with gusts of 35 to 40 mph. High\ntemperatures will be in the 30s and 40s. During the Wednesday \nnight to Thursday morning timeframe, look for a 250mb upper jet of\n110 knots bifurcating the forecast area through Thursday morning.\nSnow amounts across northern Nevada during the Wednesday to early\nThursday time frame is up to an inch in the valleys with about \none to three inches over passes and summits on area highways.\nOvernight lows will be in the 20s. Look for the snow showers to\nincrease in intensity and coverage as the upper low pressure \nsystem continues to track eastward through California and Nevada \nThursday through Friday morning. Winds in central Nevada will\ncontinue to be strong with south to southwest winds 15 to 25 mph\nwith gusts to 35 mph. This will lead to the possibility of \nblowing/drifting snow in central Nevada. With this system being\ncolder, much steeper lapse rates will help with Sierra Mountain\nspillover of moisture with widescale synoptic lift enhancing snow\nproduction. It appears the Thursday to Friday timeframe will \noffer the best chances for accumulating snow across central Nevada\nwith about two to five inches possible in the valleys with \nslightly higher amounts on area summits and passes. In northern \nNevada, up to two inches are possible in the valleys with two to \nfour inches on the higher passes, as well as in northern Elko \ncounty. Winter weather advisories may be required for this time \nframe. Highs Thursday are expected to struggle out of the 30s with\nlows in the 20s.\n\nLight snow showers are expected Friday as the main low will exit\neast. Light amounts of a half-inch can be expected. Highs will\nagain be in the 30s. Winds will be on the light side. \n\nAnother transitional ridge will build over the region this weekend\nbefore yet another short wave strengthens over the Pacific Ocean\nand impacts the Great Basin early next week. Saturday morning\ncontinues to look rather cold with lows reaching the single digits\nand teens. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Changes were made in the near\nterm in the wind, wind gust, and PoP/Wx grids versus the NBM\nforecast. Confidence is high with respect to the next system \nimpacting the region late Wednesday night through Friday. High \nconfidence exists in reaching below normal temperatures through \nthe rest of the week.",
